course: dictionary
lesson: 14358
title: The onlooker
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An onlooker is a person who watches something happen but does not take part in it. «A crowd of onlookers gathered outside the shop when the alarm rang.» The onlookers did not join in. They just stood and watched.
explain: Look at the word itself: on-looker means someone who looks on. Onlookers are often curious, so they stop to see what is going on. Another word for an onlooker is bystander, and both words describe people standing nearby.
explain: Onlookers can do more than watch. «Some onlookers ran to get a teacher when the boy fell.» If you see someone hurt or in danger, you can help by getting an adult or calling for help, instead of just watching or filming.
fact: An onlooker is a person who watches something but does not take part in it.
fact: «Onlookers» is the plural, which means more than one onlooker.
fact: «A crowd of onlookers» means many people watching the same thing.
fact: Onlookers are also called bystanders.
fact: Onlookers can help by getting an adult or calling for help.
exercise: «A crowd of onlookers gathered outside the shop.» Who are the onlookers? | People who were stealing from the shop | *People who stood and watched | Police officers who were arresting someone | People who run the shop
exercise: «The onlookers stayed back and watched the football match.» Did the onlookers play in the match? | Yes, they were the coaches | *No, they only watched | Yes, they were the goalkeepers | Yes, they were the referees
exercise: Which word means the same as onlooker? | *Bystander | Referee | Captain | Player
test: Ben saw a boy fall off his bike. He stood and filmed it on his phone and did not get help. What was Ben? | A driver who had to stop | *An onlooker who could have got help | A referee who stopped the game | A teacher on duty
test: Which sentence uses onlookers correctly? | The onlookers ran the race and won the prize | *The onlookers watched the parade from the pavement | The onlookers cooked the dinner for everyone | The onlookers scored the winning goal
exam: Why should an onlooker get an adult or call for help when someone is hurt? | Because onlookers must always run away from accidents | To make a more exciting video for others to watch | Because onlookers are not allowed to stand near the scene | *Because they can help by getting help, not just watching
